    },
    {
      "id": "dbcfd122c1c149f17dc40c1a8be802311909366b",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 2652634508,
          "type": null,
          "body": "libxml 2.13 had some pretty large API changes, and as a result not every Linux distribution has adopted it:\n\nhttps://packages.fedoraproject.org/pkgs/libxml2/libxml2/\n\nhttps://tracker.debian.org/pkg/libxml2\nhttps://release.debian.org/transitions/html/auto-libxml2.html\n\nSome of the security improvements or API changes end up breaking some existing packages. I would like to be able to require later versions, but I don't know if we can yet easily.",
          "author": {
            "id": 6764551,
            "username": "johnthacker",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "John Thacker",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/99c30adacedf45514aaf711ba8a44f56322ebb53e9b550b7e3c9cd83bd6a1408?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/johnthacker"
          },
          "created_at": "2025-07-27T18:40:55.675Z",
          "updated_at": "2025-07-27T18:40:55.675Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 402541588,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": false,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 20665,
          "commands_changes": {}
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "eb502b4452f6293a45592e8f0ec52881a7459e4c",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 2652658765,
          "type": null,
          "body": "Are we then susceptible to some of those security issues?  Requiring libxml2 is new to Wireshark so I was hoping we could require a later version, but since we're using it to replace existing functionality, I guess it turns into a backwards compatibility issue (if distros lose functionality previously done by \"quick and dirty parsers\".)\nI hadn't been paying attention to the API I used in addressing #20508  so far because it was certainly passing the pipeline (and wasn't exactly handling complex XML).  This MR may provide a good example of what I was trying to achieve (originally, I didn't look at the SAX API directly).  And I'll certainly tag @johnthacker on an MR if I get something working.",
          "author": {
            "id": 8399509,
            "username": "mmann78",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Michael Mann",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/fad119898ba16a3f8be2f765dcff399098444abd4f85a04d4c3ef415a2bbe3d9?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/mmann78"
          },
          "created_at": "2025-07-27T20:01:16.137Z",
          "updated_at": "2025-07-27T20:01:16.137Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 402541588,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": false,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 20665,
          "commands_changes": {}
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "d8b9a0b4584ba39755d919930a53bc5063534841",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 2653138756,
          "type": null,
          "body": "The security issues with XML that I'm familiar with (and that libxml2 can prevent by either giving or not giving certain parser options) are repeated substitution of entities to create huge documents/denial of service (\"billion laughs\"), and various vulnerabilities to do with external entities. The latter can including going out onto the network to retrieve data (e.g. via built in HTTP or FTP clients) or loading files or other commands locally. In addition, libxml2 is concerned about denial of service that occurs from automatically decompressing source files if those files are zip bombs or the like. (Wireshark is already vulnerable to that, including if compression bombs are created and used as HTTP payload and then parsed by the HTTP dissector, for example.)\n\nThe worst security impacts with these flaws occur when a server processes untrusted XML that is provided by a client. We're probably less vulnerable than those types of systems, but at the least it seems like it would be possible to cause Wireshark to hang trying to load a bad file with certain parser options.",
          "author": {
            "id": 6764551,
            "username": "johnthacker",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "John Thacker",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/99c30adacedf45514aaf711ba8a44f56322ebb53e9b550b7e3c9cd83bd6a1408?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/johnthacker"
          },
          "created_at": "2025-07-28T06:51:10.689Z",
          "updated_at": "2025-07-28T06:51:10.689Z",
